        "Your phone arrives in a runner's hand before you reach the tram, wiped, warm, as if it had been held. You do not check it under the lobby lights. You walk out into the scheduled rain and let the grid wash the vetiver down to a rumor.\n\nThe deck is the same deck as a hundred other nights, leaking ads, civic melancholy, trains moving slow. You are not the same. Your thigh remembers a palm. Your mouth remembers a thumb or a refusal or a lesson you paid for in a hotel and spent in an inner room. The city does not care which.\n\nA man vomits neatly into a planter. A woman laughs into a phone in a language the ads have not bought yet. You stand under the CHRYSALIS loop and watch a throat rewrite itself until the loop resets, and you understand the ad better than you did an hour ago.",
        "Voss will want a debrief. She will want it tonight, because delay is how assets grow second lives. You can already hear her voice, paper cup, rain, the subtraction. You can tell her Crowe knows the name. You can tell her about the memo's three verbs. You can keep the hand and the glass and the smoked pupil to yourself, a private filth that is also a private power.\n\nOr you can lie. Lying to Voss only gets easier after the first time. Or you can delay until morning and let the night settle into a shape you can carry without spilling.\n\nThe tram arrives. People practice being unremarkable. You practice with them. Your badge says Rivers. Your mouth, if you close your eyes, still says {{name}} the way Crowe said it: like a leash he was willing to make pretty.",
