        "You take the last car. You call because not calling would be a confession. You give her the launch. Investor nervousness. A senator-shaped hole in the gala plan. Crowe as a professional who likes late meetings. You make it sound like work. You make your voice Rivers.\n\nVoss lets you finish. The silence afterward is a hand on a thigh you did not mention.\n\n\"You're keeping something,\" she says. Not a question. \"Keep it if it keeps you walking. Don't keep it if it is a hook in your mouth you have started to like. I can't pull a hook I can't see.\"",
        "You tell her you understand. Understanding is a lie that sounds like a skill. She gives you the next beat anyway, because she is still trying to be a handler and not a eulogy. \"There is a document. Three verbs. I want photographs, not stories. Special Projects records annex, the floor that smells like warm dust. Marek Pell walks that floor like he owns the lint. If you see him, you are already late.\"\n\nShe hangs up before you can put more furniture in the lie. The tram window returns your face, slightly wrong. You touch your own lip and then stop, because the gesture is becoming a habit.",
